## Description

The chat becomes noticeably laggy when scrolling through sessions with a large number of messages. This is most apparent when scrolling up through older messages or when the conversation has grown over many turns.

## Root Cause

In `ChatTranscriptView.swift`, the message list renders inside a `ScrollView` with a regular `VStack`:

```swift
ScrollView {
    VStack(spacing: transcriptMessageSpacing) {
        ForEach(displayedTranscriptMessages) { ... }
    }
}
```

A `VStack` keeps **every** message view alive in memory simultaneously, even ones scrolled far off screen. Each message is heavyweight — it passes through `MarkdownRenderer`, `MarkdownMathSegmenter`, `Highlightr` (syntax highlighting), `StreamingTextFade` animations, and `@AppStorage` reads. With hundreds of messages, that's hundreds of complex views all maintained in memory at once.

## Suggested Fix

Switch to `LazyVStack` so SwiftUI only keeps on-screen views alive:

```swift
ScrollView {
    LazyVStack(spacing: transcriptMessageSpacing) {
        ForEach(displayedTranscriptMessages) { ... }
    }
}
```

On iOS 17+, pair with `.scrollPosition(id:)` instead of `ScrollViewProxy.scrollTo()` — the modern API works correctly with lazy stacks where views are destroyed and recreated.

**Note:** I understand the current scroll management is sophisticated (`ChatScrollPolicy`, `ChatScrollObserver`, auto-follow-bottom, position-preserving older-message loads), and `LazyVStack` can sometimes fight with `ScrollViewReader` proxies. The iOS 17+ scroll position API should handle this cleanly, but it may require careful testing of the streaming follow-scroll and pull-to-refresh-position-preservation flows.

## Impact

This would be a significant quality-of-life improvement for any session that runs long. The current behavior effectively degrades linearly with message count.

## Use case

Hermex has an excellent native UX, but currently requires every user to install and operate [`nesquena/hermes-webui`](https://github.com/nesquena/hermes-webui) as its server, even when they already run the official [Nous Research Hermes Agent](https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent) Gateway.

That is a heavy architectural dependency for a mobile client:

- it introduces a second, third-party server implementation between Hermex and Hermes Agent;
- users must deploy, secure, authenticate, monitor and update another network service;
- its API contract and release cadence are independent of official Hermes Agent;
- Hermex must pin and continuously track that third-party contract;
- it raises the barrier to trying Hermex for the existing Hermes Agent user base.

Direct Gateway support could substantially enlarge Hermex's addressable user base. People already running `hermes gateway` could install the iOS app, provide their Gateway URL and API key, and immediately obtain a strong native mobile experience without deploying a second backend.

Issues [#172](https://github.com/uzairansaruzi/hermex/issues/172) and [#176](https://github.com/uzairansaruzi/hermex/issues/176) already show demand for compatibility with official `hermes-agent`, although those reports concern the built-in dashboard contract rather than direct use of the Gateway API.

## Proposed behavior

Add an alternative server/backend option, for example **Hermes Agent Gateway API**, alongside the existing **Hermes WebUI** option.

A Gateway connection would use:

- the Gateway base URL, normally port `8642`;
- bearer authentication through `API_SERVER_KEY`;
- `GET /v1/capabilities` for version-safe feature discovery;
- capability-gated UI so unsupported features are hidden or clearly marked unavailable.

This does not need full WebUI parity in its first release. A useful initial implementation could cover:

1. health and capability detection;
2. chat and persistent runs using `/v1/runs` and SSE events;
3. run status, stop and approval handling;
4. session listing, history, creation, rename, deletion, fork and streamed chat through `/api/sessions`;
5. scheduled-job listing and management through `/api/jobs`;
6. skill and toolset discovery through `/v1/skills` and `/v1/toolsets`.

The existing WebUI backend could remain fully supported and continue unlocking Hermex's richer features. Features without an official Gateway equivalent—such as workspace/Git browsing, memory management, file uploads, TTS/transcription or some profile/model controls—could remain **WebUI only** until the official API exposes suitable contracts.

A transport abstraction would allow both backends without forcing lowest-common-denominator behavior:

```text
Hermex UI
  ├── HermesWebUIBackend          existing full feature set
  └── HermesAgentGatewayBackend   official capability-gated baseline
```

## Why the Gateway API is now a practical target

The official Hermes Agent API Server is no longer merely a stateless OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint. Its documented feature set now includes:

- `/v1/capabilities` for machine-readable feature negotiation;
- Chat Completions and Responses APIs;
- persistent runs with detachable SSE progress streams;
- run polling, cancellation and approval responses;
- session CRUD, message history, branching and synchronous/streamed chat;
- scheduled-job CRUD, pause/resume and immediate execution;
- skill and toolset discovery;
- bearer-token authentication and detailed health/readiness reporting.

This is enough for a credible, useful Hermex baseline even though it does not yet cover every WebUI feature.

Official API documentation: [Hermes Agent API Server](https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/docs/user-guide/features/api-server/)

## Acceptance outcomes

- Hermex can connect directly to an official Hermes Agent Gateway using a URL and `API_SERVER_KEY`.
- Connection setup probes `/health` and `/v1/capabilities` and records the available feature set.
- Basic chat/run streaming and cancellation work without `hermes-webui` being installed.
- Available session and job operations are exposed when advertised by the Gateway.
- Unsupported capabilities are hidden or labelled as requiring the WebUI backend; they do not cause connection or decoding failures.
- Existing Hermes WebUI users retain the current full feature set and behavior.
- Tests cover both backend contracts independently.

Even a deliberately limited first version would remove the largest adoption barrier for existing Hermes Agent users while preserving WebUI as the richer optional integration.

## Summary

PR #298 introduced durable app-owned copies for staged draft attachments and correctly added cleanup on successful sends, explicit removal, session deletion, server removal, and unreferenced-orphan sweeps. Retained attachment storage itself is still unbounded.

## Current source

Current `master` at `1a9f409b192aecaf443997d4e7b06b41dd3f6305`:

- each attachment may be up to 20 MB (`HermesMobile/Models/UploadResponse.swift:134-139`);
- `ChatDraftStore` has no per-draft attachment count/byte cap or global retained-byte ceiling (`HermesMobile/Persistence/ChatDraftStore.swift:462-486`);
- `sweepOrphanedAttachmentFiles()` passes every referenced filename to the attachment store, so referenced files are exempt regardless of age (`ChatDraftStore.swift:681-694`);
- explicit cleanup now occurs for deleted sessions and removed servers, but an abandoned new-chat draft or long-lived session draft can retain its copies indefinitely.

A user can therefore accumulate an unbounded number of referenced files across drafts. The orphan sweep cannot reclaim them, and eventual Application Support exhaustion makes future attachment staging fail.

## Required policy

Choose a bounded retention contract without silently deleting referenced user data. A complete design should define:

1. per-draft attachment count and retained-byte limits enforced before durable save;
2. a global retained-byte ceiling across all servers and draft contexts;
3. eviction/expiry behavior for referenced abandoned drafts, including user-visible warning or an explicit cleanup surface;
4. deterministic ordering (for example last-updated draft/file), multi-server isolation, and restart-stable metadata;
5. shared-reference safety so deleting one draft never removes a copy still referenced elsewhere;
6. behavior when the cap is reached: fail staging before network upload or require explicit user cleanup, rather than silently dropping an existing draft.

## Acceptance tests

- one file exactly at the limit succeeds; over-limit write performs zero durable/network side effects;
- per-draft count and byte limits are both enforced;
- global cap composes across two servers and new-chat/session contexts;
- referenced-file eviction never happens silently;
- explicit cleanup or acknowledged eviction updates the draft document and file store atomically enough to survive relaunch;
- session delete, server removal, successful direct/queued send, explicit attachment removal, and orphan sweep retain their existing cleanup behavior;
- a copy referenced by two records is deleted only after the final reference is removed;
- migration from the version-2 document remains lossless.

## Relationship

Post-merge follow-up to #298. The user-visible data-loss and settings-ownership findings were fixed before merge; this issue tracks only the retained-storage budget/product policy that remained unresolved.

## Summary

Concurrent foreground recovery calls are not serialized on current master (`99c19151880e3b296c61c35a034dc04ae06731af`). `ChatStreamCoordinator.reconnectIfNeeded()` can launch overlapping status checks and transcript reloads for the same suspended run.

The complete design and regression matrix existed in closed PR #175 by @TheEpTic, but that branch is now stale. It was closed with “fixed in #295”; source verification shows #295 addresses late SSE/terminal-event ownership and does **not** add reconnect single-flight ownership.

## Current source

`HermesMobile/Features/Chat/ChatStreamCoordinator.swift:250-298`:

- has no `reconnectTask` / recovery request identity;
- each caller independently captures the stream/generation and calls `chatStreamStatus`;
- each active/inactive response may independently enter `streamCoordinatorLoadMessages`;
- only post-load stream/suspension guards arbitrate settlement;
- one caller with a real `ModelContext` cannot upgrade an earlier in-flight caller that started with nil.

The active-path guards reduce some double-start outcomes, but they do not prevent duplicate status/load work or establish one caller/one context as the recovery owner.

## Reproduction shape

Use deterministic barriers against the real coordinator:

1. Start `stream-123`, then suspend it.
2. Call `reconnectIfNeeded()` and hold the first status response.
3. Call `reconnectIfNeeded(modelContext: nonNilContext)` again.
4. Current behavior starts a second status request rather than joining the first.
5. Release both and observe duplicate recovery work / context ownership ambiguity.

Also cover a new stream or same stream with a bumped generation replacing the in-flight recovery before the first status/load settles.

## Proposed implementation

Port the sound mechanism from #175 onto current master rather than merging the stale branch wholesale:

- one stored recovery task keyed by task ID, stream ID, and `runGeneration`;
- concurrent callers join it;
- a later nonnil `ModelContext` upgrades the operation before transcript load;
- identity/generation checks after every await;
- invalidate/cancel on `start`, new-response preparation, completion, `finishStream`, and session-load replacement;
- an older task must never clear or finalize a successor;
- cancellation should stop redundant underlying work where possible, not only clear the task reference.

## Acceptance tests

- two concurrent reconnect calls issue exactly one status request and one transcript load;
- joining nonnil ModelContext is used by the shared load;
- stream replacement starts one new recovery and the old task cannot mutate it;
- same stream ID with new generation is independently owned;
- session load replacing the stream invalidates the old recovery;
- prior stream end + new loaded stream starts a fresh recovery;
- active, replay, inactive, missing-stream, and error paths preserve existing semantics;
- full XCTest + exact Build/Test/CI Gate pass.

## Attribution

If #175's implementation/tests are ported, preserve @TheEpTic as co-author and credit the original PR in the new PR body. Do not attribute this design to #295.

## Summary

After a successful nonempty `/api/models/live` refresh for a provider, the matched provider group's selectable `allModels` can still include models that were removed from the authoritative live response.

Current master: `75211ad079b61d4565bdb763623f67f6e82cdb6e`

## Root cause

`ModelCatalogGroup.allModels` correctly returns `models + extraModels`, deduplicated by ID.

But `Array<ModelCatalogGroup>.mergingLiveModels(from:)` replaces only `models` and preserves the cached `extraModels` tail:

```swift
ModelCatalogGroup(
    ...,
    models: liveModels,
    extraModels: group.extraModels
)
```

The method's own comment says the nonempty live list is authoritative for additions and removals. Once pickers/favorites/title lookup use `allModels`, preserved cached overflow IDs remain visible/selectable even when the live provider no longer returns them.

This was surfaced while reviewing PR #330. That PR is superseded by already-merged #293 and should not become the fix vehicle.

## Required contract

For the matched provider after a valid, nonempty live response:

- `allModels` must contain exactly the live provider's IDs, once each;
- removed cached featured or overflow IDs must disappear;
- new live IDs must appear once;
- unrelated provider groups remain byte-equivalent;
- empty/degenerate/mismatched live responses retain the cached group, preserving current fail-safe behavior.

If the UI must preserve a bounded featured subset, repartition the authoritative live list explicitly:

1. retain surviving previously-featured IDs in their live order;
2. fill remaining featured slots from the live list up to the prior featured count;
3. put the remaining live IDs in `extraModels`;
4. never carry an ID absent from the live response.

Do not simply keep stale `extraModels`, and avoid rendering every provider model eagerly if the featured/overflow UX remains intentional.

## Acceptance tests

- cached featured `[A, B]`, overflow `[C, D]`, live `[A, C, E]` → `allModels == [A, C, E]`, no B/D;
- live duplicate IDs deduplicate deterministically;
- live response order/labels/provider IDs are authoritative;
- selected/favorited removed models are no longer offered as catalog options and fall back through the existing custom/missing-selection behavior;
- unrelated groups unchanged;
- empty/missing provider and unusable live rows preserve cache;
- composer picker, Settings picker, compact menu, favorites/recents, selected title, slash autocomplete, and draft restore all agree.

## Relationship

- #293 shipped full-catalog picker/lookup parity.
- #330 duplicates/subsets that shipped work and is conflicting; close it as superseded.
- This issue owns only authoritative live-replacement semantics.

